  "domainDescriptions": {
    "Interoception": "Helps us to know what is going on within our bodies, to identify if we are hungry, thirsty, hot/cold, in pain or needing the toilet etc. We can be over or under sensitive or have difficulty with discrimination. Interoception links very closely to our ability to self-regulate and to alexithymia (Identifying and understanding our emotions).",
    "Proprioception": "Body awareness, tells us where the different parts of our body are without looking at them. Also helps us to know how much pressure or force we need to use for different activities. It is more uncommon for someone to be over responsive to proprioceptive input.",
    "Vestibular": "The movement, balance and gravity sense. Allows us to move smoothly, maintain balance and stay upright.",
    "Tactility (touch)": "Tactile receptors are mainly found in the skin and are important for identifying touch, pressure, pain, temperature and texture.",
    "Vision (sight)": "Sight is processed through the visual sense. Visual receptors located in the eye pick up visual stimuli. They are stimulated by light colour and movement. A  CYP can feels unsafe or unsure in the environment if over stimulated visually or can feel anxious if they cannot interpret their environment.",
    "Auditory (hearing)": "The ability to detect and process sound. A CYP may be over-sensitive to noise, easily distracted by background sounds, or under-sensitive and seek louder or more intense auditory input.",
    "Gustatory (taste)": "The sense of taste. A CYP may be strongly avoidant of certain flavours or textures in the mouth, or may seek intense tastes and show little sensitivity to what they eat.",
    "Olfactory (smell)": "The sense of smell. A CYP may be overly sensitive to everyday odours, finding them overwhelming or distressing, or may seek out strong smells and show little reaction to unpleasant ones."
  },
